.TH TIME 2
.SH NAME
time, ftime \- get date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.B time_t time(time_t *tloc);
.PP
.B #include <sys/timeb.h>
.PP
.B int ftime(struct timeb *tp);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Time
returns the time since 00:00:00 GMT, Jan. 1, 1970, measured
in seconds.
.PP
If
.I tloc
is nonnull,
the return value is also stored in the
place to which
.I tloc
points.
.PP
The
.I ftime
entry fills in a structure pointed to by its argument,
as defined by
.IR <sys/timeb.h> :
.PP
.nf
.ta .5i +\w'unsigned 'u
struct timeb {
	time_t	time;
	int	millitm;
	int	timezone;
	int	dstflag;
};
.fi
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page pulled this in with a
.B .so
of the installed header; it is written out here, there being no /usr/include on
this system.
.PP
The structure contains the time since the epoch in seconds,
up to 1000 milliseconds of more-precise interval,
the local timezone (measured in minutes of time westward from Greenwich),
and a flag that, if nonzero, indicates that
Daylight Saving time applies locally during the appropriate part of the year.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
date(1), stime(2), ctime(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B time_t is one word,
41 bits of seconds, so there is no 2038 problem \- and no second word for the gate
to carry, which is why
.IR stime (2)
lost an argument.
.PP
.B The gate takes no argument at all.
The time comes back in the accumulator and the store through
.I tloc
is the stub's own doing, which is why this stub is hand-written rather than
generated.  See
.IR intro (2).
.PP
.B Ftime is where the zone comes from,
as it did in v7:
.IR localtime (3),
.I mktime
and
.IR strftime 's
.B %z
and
.B %Z
all ask it.  The kernel owns the westward offset and the daylight flag, and there
is no zoneinfo to read.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_time,
.B SYS_ftime
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
